. * GENERATE VARIABLES * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         *Explanatory variables
.                 
.                 * Count the number of daughters per respondent / observation
.                 egen num_daughters = anycount(kidsex01-kidsex19), values(2)

.                 
.                 * Generate dummy variable for whether participant has at leas
> t one daughter (1) or not (0)
.                 gen many_daughters=0

.                 replace many_daughters=1 if num_daughters!=0
(277,922 real changes made)

.                 
.                 
.                 
.         * Outcome variables
.         
.                 * Merging outcomes: plh0308_v1 and plh0308_v2 as they reflect
>  our best understanding of gender equality (i.e., they reflect attitudes towa
> rds genders: gender equality in work and family)
.                 
.                 * Treat observations with -8 (Question this year not part of 
> the survey), -5 (Not included in this version) and -1 (No answer) as missing:
.                 replace plh0308_v1=. if plh0308_v1<0
(499,783 real changes made, 499,783 to missing)

.                 replace plh0308_v2=. if plh0308_v2<0
(482,363 real changes made, 482,363 to missing)

.                 
.                 /* Rearrange categories for plh0308_v1 (scale is the opposite
>  as plh0308_v2)
>                 i.e., plh0308_v1: Man/woman are equal in work and household/f
> amily; scale 1 completely agree to 4 completely disagree
>                 plh0308_v2: Best if man and woman work the same amount so the
> y can share the responsibility; scale 1 completely disagree to 7 completely a
> gree*/
.                 replace plh0308_v1 = 5 - plh0308_v1 if !missing(plh0308_v1)
(6,688 real changes made)

.                 
.                 * Rescale so that both variables have the same number of pote
> ntial answers
.                 gen plh0308_v2_rescaled = .
(573,844 missing values generated)

.                 replace plh0308_v2_rescaled = 1 if inlist(plh0308_v2, 1, 2)  
>     // Strongly disagree
(1,858 real changes made)

.                 replace plh0308_v2_rescaled = 2 if inlist(plh0308_v2, 3, 4)  
>     // Somewhat disagree  
(5,284 real changes made)

.                 replace plh0308_v2_rescaled = 3 if inlist(plh0308_v2, 5, 6)  
>     // Somewhat agree
(6,949 real changes made)

.                 replace plh0308_v2_rescaled = 4 if plh0308_v2 == 7           
>    // Strongly agree
(10,017 real changes made)

. 
.                 
.                 * Merge both into a new variable
.                 gen gender_equality_attitude = .
(573,844 missing values generated)

.                 replace gender_equality_attitude = plh0308_v1 if !missing(plh
> 0308_v1)
(6,688 real changes made)

.                 replace gender_equality_attitude = plh0308_v2_rescaled if !mi
> ssing(plh0308_v2)
(24,108 real changes made)

.         
.         
. 
. 
. 
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         /* Assuming that parents do not follow a gender-based fertility inter
> ruption rule (like the one-child policy in China, for example), we assume tha
> t, once respondents have a child, the sex of the child cannot be chosen. 
>         This allows us to use a simple OLS regression to analyze the effects 
> of having at least one daughter on gender equality (as measured by attitudes)
> , after controlling for the number of kids (sumkids)
>         Since this question is only asked in the survey from 2018, we do not 
> consider that we should control for a time trend.*/
. 
.         reg gender_equality_attitude many_daughters sumkids //The higher the 
> coefficient the more gender equal

      Source |       SS           df       MS      Number of obs   =    30,796
-------------+----------------------------------   F(2, 30793)     =     71.29
       Model |  130.720464         2   65.360232   Prob > F        =    0.0000
